# Emotet (2014 onwards). A loader spread by Word attachments in replies to real email threads; it installed other malware.
# A quest (QUESTS.md): the trainee builds a budget pack, checks one real attachment safely, then meets an Emotet reply in a thread of their own.
os windows
user Dana Okafor
about
  | Emotet replied to real email conversations it had stolen, with a Word file that asked the reader to turn on editing and content. The name on the message was a colleague's, and it quoted words the reader had really written. Its macro downloaded Emotet, which stole more email threads and installed the next malware, often ransomware. This training copy is invented: Northwind Traders is not a real company.
story
  | You are Dana Okafor, finance coordinator at Northwind Traders. This week you are building the Q4 budget pack: each department sends its figures in a file, and you check every one before it goes in.
  | It is Monday morning. You have checked 3 of the 5 department files. Two to go. On Thursday you also sent Marcus, in Operations, your draft and asked him to check the travel line.
  | Your mailbox password is in a text file on the desktop so you do not have to retype it. That is a bad habit, kept here only to make the training quick.
quota 3 5 Q4 budget files checked

# Dana's desk: the details she copies from (a soft flag: careless, but only here to keep the training simple).
file Desktop/mailbox-login.txt
  at 930 200
  | Northwind mail
  | Address: dana.okafor@northwind.example
  | Password: Maple-Quill-2026

# Thursday's real message: the thread that Emotet will reuse.
mail draft-sent
  folder sent
  sent 2026-10-29 09:41
  from Dana Okafor <dana.okafor@northwind.example>
  to Marcus Lee <marcus.lee@northwind.example>
  subject Budget draft for Q4
  | Marcus, here is my first draft. Can you check the travel line?

# The safe task: a real attachment from a real colleague. The figures show at once; nothing has to be turned on.
view budget-ok sheet
  title Marketing_Q4_lines.xlsx [Protected View]
  app Spreadsheet
  size 760x520
  bar PROTECTED VIEW  The figures are already showing: you can check them without editing.
  section Marketing: Q4 lines
  columns Line | Q3 actual | Q4 plan
  cells Advertising | 18,400 | 19,000
  cells Events | 7,250 | 8,500
  cells Print | 2,100 | 1,800
  cells Agency fees | 12,000 | 12,000
  cells Total | 39,750 | 41,300
program budget-ok Marketing_Q4_lines.xlsx
  publisher none
  signed no
  description Budget figures
  view budget-ok
mail marketing
  from Priya Shah <priya.shah@northwind.example>
  subject RE: Q4 marketing lines
  attach Downloads/Marketing_Q4_lines.xlsx
  | Hi Dana,
  |
  | Marketing's Q4 figures are attached, as agreed last week. Events is up a little for the spring launch.
  |
  | Priya
file Downloads/Marketing_Q4_lines.xlsx
  program budget-ok
  size 31744

step Budget file 4 of 5
  look Mail: Priya's message with the figures attached
  | A new file has come in from Priya, in Marketing. It is the one you were waiting for. Check it the normal way, so you know what normal looks like.
notify mail Priya Shah
  | RE: Q4 marketing lines

step A colleague you know
  look the sender's address and the attachment under the message
  when mail marketing
  cue file:fs-marketing-q4-lines-xlsx-f703ae43 mail:marketing app:mail
  | The message is from Priya's own Northwind address, answers something you agreed last week, and the file is a spreadsheet of figures. Hover over a sender to check the address behind the name.
open marketing

step Read the figures
  look the spreadsheet: the figures, and the counter in the guide
  when run budget-ok
  | The spreadsheet opens in Protected View, which stops anything inside from running. The figures are on the screen straight away and the totals add up: 4 of 5 files done. A real budget never needs more than that to be read.
  | One file to go. And Marcus has just answered your draft.
open Downloads/Marketing_Q4_lines.xlsx
quota +1

# The lure: a reply in the thread Dana started on Thursday. Real words, a stranger's address, a Word file that asks for permission.
view emotet-doc document
  title Budget_Q4_0412.doc [Protected View] - Word
  app Document
  size 760x520
  page
  bar PROTECTED VIEW  Be careful: files from the Internet can contain viruses. Unless you need to edit, it is safer to stay in Protected View. -> Enable Editing -> next
  icon lock
  heading This document was created in an earlier version.
  | To view or edit this document, please click the "Enable editing" button on the top bar, and then click "Enable content".
  | Training copy: there is no budget in this file and no real macro.
  page
  bar SECURITY WARNING  Macros have been disabled. -> Enable Content -> run:emotet-powershell
  icon lock
  heading This document was created in an earlier version.
  | To view or edit this document, please click the "Enable editing" button on the top bar, and then click "Enable content".
  | Training copy: there is no budget in this file and no real macro.

# The macro starts PowerShell hidden. Word is its parent. Nothing is downloaded here: the desktop has no network.
program emotet-powershell Windows PowerShell
  publisher Microsoft Corporation
  signed yes
  process powershell.exe
  cpu 22
  mem 74
  parent WINWORD.EXE
  command powershell.exe -w hidden -enc JABzAD0A...

# Emotet itself, run by PowerShell. It copies itself to AppData and adds a Run key.
program emotet-loader 581.exe
  publisher none
  signed no
  process 581.exe
  cpu 8
  mem 19
  parent powershell.exe
  command C:\Users\Dana\AppData\Local\581.exe
  startup 581 C:\Users\Dana\AppData\Local\581.exe
  location HKCU\Software\Microsoft\Windows\CurrentVersion\Run

# The Word attachment: a .doc with a macro. Opening it shows Protected View.
program emotet-word Budget_Q4_0412.doc
  publisher none
  signed no
  command "C:\Program Files\Microsoft Office\root\Office16\WINWORD.EXE" "Budget_Q4_0412.doc"
  view emotet-doc
mail reply
  from Marcus Lee <accounts@harbour-print.invalid>
  subject RE: Budget draft for Q4
  attach Downloads/Budget_Q4_0412.doc
  | Hi Dana, please see the updated figures attached.
  |
  | > From: Dana Okafor
  | > Sent: Thursday 09:41
  | > Subject: Budget draft for Q4
  | > Marcus, here is my first draft. Can you check the travel line?
file Downloads/Budget_Q4_0412.doc
  program emotet-word
  size 214016
notify mail Marcus Lee
  | RE: Budget draft for Q4

step A reply to your own draft
  look the sender line: a name Dana knows, an address she does not
  when mail reply
  cue file:fs-budget-q4-0412-doc-2e95f9dd mail:reply app:mail
  | It answers the question you asked Marcus on Thursday, quoting your own words. Marcus's PC was infected earlier, so Emotet copied your thread with him. The reply comes from another mailbox that Emotet had taken over, so it looks like the next mail in a conversation you started.
  | The name is Marcus's. The address behind it is a stranger's mailbox. This time the file is a Word document, not figures. Flag anything that looks wrong.
open reply

step The document asks you to turn things on
  look Word: the yellow Protected View bar and the “earlier version” page
  when run emotet-word
  move Do what the page says: click “Enable Editing” in the yellow bar.
  | Word opens the file in Protected View, which stops macros. Compare it with the spreadsheet: there are no figures, only an excuse and an arrow to the bar.
  | A real budget never needs Enable Editing to be read.
open Downloads/Budget_Q4_0412.doc

step Enable Editing
  look the bar: it now says Security Warning, with “Enable Content”
  when page emotet-doc 2
  move Click “Enable Content” in the Security Warning bar.
  | Pressing Enable Editing took the file out of Protected View. Word now says macros are disabled and offers “Enable Content”, which is what starts the macro.
press emotet-doc next

step The macro starts PowerShell
  look the process list: powershell.exe, busy on the CPU, and the “Connection blocked” notification
  when run emotet-powershell
  | “Enable Content” runs the macro hidden in the file. It starts PowerShell with an encoded command (-enc), so nobody reading it sees what it does.
  | The command downloads Emotet. Here that download is blocked: the training desktop has no network.
press emotet-doc run:emotet-powershell
open taskmanager
block cdn-files.invalid 443

step Emotet installs itself
  look 581.exe in the process list and in the start-up list
  | On a real PC the download works: 581.exe lands in AppData and starts. The Word window never shows a budget, so Dana thinks the file is broken and carries on.
  | It adds itself to the start-up list, so a restart does not remove it.
folder Home/AppData
folder Home/AppData/Local
file Home/AppData/Local/581.exe
  program emotet-loader
  size 392192
launch emotet-loader
open taskmanager

# Back to work: the last department file, a genuine one, while Emotet reads the mailbox.
page pack-added budget-pack.northwind.example/q4/added
  title Q4 budget pack: file added
  brand Northwind Q4 Budget Pack
  heading HR lines added
  | The HR lines are in the Q4 pack. 5 of 5 department files are done.
page pack-back budget-pack.northwind.example/q4/returned
  title Q4 budget pack: sent back
  brand Northwind Q4 Budget Pack
  heading HR lines sent back
  | The HR lines go back to Priya for another look.
page pack-hr budget-pack.northwind.example/q4/hr-lines
  title Q4 budget pack: HR lines
  brand Northwind Q4 Budget Pack
  heading HR lines: ready to add
  | Priya has put the HR figures straight into the budget pack.
  section HR: Q4 lines
  row Training = 6,200
  row Recruitment = 4,500
  row Total = 10,700
  button Add to the Q4 pack -> pack-added
  button Send back to Priya -> pack-back
mail hr-mail
  from Priya Shah <priya.shah@northwind.example>
  subject Last one: HR lines for the Q4 pack
  link pack-hr
  | Dana,
  |
  | HR's figures are the last ones. I put them in the budget pack: the link opens them, ready to add.
  |
  | Priya
notify mail Priya Shah
  | Last one: HR lines for the Q4 pack

step Back to work
  look Mail: Priya's last message
  when mail hr-mail
  | Priya's last file of the week. This one is a link to the company's own budget pack, not an attachment.
open hr-mail

step The link goes to the pack
  look the link under Priya's message
  when visit pack-hr
  | The link goes to budget-pack.northwind.example, the same address as all week.
open pack-hr

step Check, then add
  look the HR figures and the Add button
  when choose pack-hr any
  move Check the figures, then click “Add to the Q4 pack”.
  | The page is the same pack you have used all week. The figures add up: 6,200 and 4,500 make 10,700. That makes 5 of 5 files. Then Priya writes to you.
choose pack-hr 1
quota +1
mail priya-asks
  from Priya Shah <priya.shah@northwind.example>
  subject Did you send me this?
  sent 2026-11-02 09:33
  | Dana, I just got a reply from you to the Q3 review thread saying “please see the updated figures attached”. I did not ask for anything. Did you send it?
  |
  | Priya
notify mail Priya Shah
  | Did you send me this?

step Did you send me this?
  look Mail: Priya's question, then the Sent folder
  when mail priya-asks
  | Priya asks whether you sent her a reply to the Q3 thread. You did not write it.
  | The Sent folder has three replies you never wrote, all with the same words: “please see the updated figures attached”.
mail sent-1
  folder sent
  sent 2026-11-02 09:21
  from Dana Okafor <dana.okafor@northwind.example>
  to Priya Shah <priya.shah@northwind.example>
  subject RE: Q3 report review
  attach Downloads/Budget_Q4_0412.doc
  | Hi Priya, please see the updated figures attached.
mail sent-2
  folder sent
  sent 2026-11-02 09:22
  from Dana Okafor <dana.okafor@northwind.example>
  to Marcus Lee <marcus.lee@northwind.example>
  subject RE: Holiday photos list
  attach Downloads/Budget_Q4_0412.doc
  | Hi Marcus, please see the updated figures attached.
mail sent-3
  folder sent
  sent 2026-11-02 09:24
  from Dana Okafor <dana.okafor@northwind.example>
  to Priya Shah <priya.shah@northwind.example>
  subject RE: Notes from Monday
  attach Downloads/Budget_Q4_0412.doc
  | Hi Priya, please see the updated figures attached.
open sent-1

# The reveal: what Emotet did with the mailbox while Dana finished her shift.
step What Emotet did with the mailbox
  look the Sent folder: the replies Dana never wrote
  | Emotet had copied Dana's mail threads and her mail password. It logged in as her and sent the same Word file to her contacts, as replies to real threads, so the mail is believed.
  | Then it sells the access: on real networks the next program was often ransomware.
notify mail Mail threads copied
  | Emotet read the inbox and sent the conversations to the attackers, to reuse in its next emails.
attack mass-mail-worm
  | Dana's mail threads copied
  | “Updated figures” sent to Priya
  | “Updated figures” sent to Marcus
  | access sold on: ransomware next
block 203.0.113.61 8080

flag hard mail reply
  | The name is Marcus's, but the address is a stranger's mailbox at harbour-print.invalid, not Northwind's.
flag hard file Downloads/Budget_Q4_0412.doc
  | An old-format Word document where figures were expected. It shows no budget, only an excuse and an arrow to the bar.
flag hard process powershell.exe
  | Windows PowerShell, busy on the CPU, started by the Word macro. It ran a hidden, encoded command (-enc).
flag hard process 581.exe
  | Emotet running under a number, from the AppData folder.
flag hard startup 581
  | Emotet added itself to the start-up list, so it starts every time Dana signs in.
flag hard mail sent-1
  | A reply Dana never wrote: Emotet used her stolen mail password to send it.
flag soft file Desktop/mailbox-login.txt
  | A mail password in a text file on the desktop: any program that runs can read it, and Emotet's modules also took saved mail passwords. Use a password manager.

lesson
  | A reply in a thread you know can still come from an attacker. Check the address behind the name, not only the name and the quoted words.
  | Never click “Enable Editing” or “Enable Content” to read a document. A real budget shows its figures in Protected View. If an attachment looks wrong, ask the sender another way: phone them or write a new message.
  | If you opened one by mistake, tell IT at once, change your mail password, and do not keep working. Block macros from the internet and use two-step sign-in, so a stolen mailbox cannot be used alone.
